User-visible literal, MergeRequestLink.swift:17-18:
```
/// How each forge writes its own: "!95" on GitLab, "#95" everywhere else.
var label: String { forge == .gitlab ? "!\(number)" : "#\(number)" }
```
`label` is genuinely user-visible: LinkPopover.swift:80 `Text("\(Text("Merge").fontWeight(.semibold)) \(Text(verbatim: request.label))")`; HydraMergePopover.swift:131 `Text(verbatim: "\(note.link.label) on \(host)")`; AppModel+HydraMerge.swift:277, :278, :310, :311. `title` (MergeRequestLink.swift:42) is `"Merge \(label)"`.
URL parsing, MergeRequestLink.swift:23-39, keyed on exactly three path segments. Note the three branches are NOT symmetric:
```
let parts = url.pathComponents.filter { $0 != "/" }
if let index = parts.firstIndex(of: "merge_requests"), index >= 1, index + 1 < parts.count,
   let number = Int(parts[index + 1]), number > 0 {           // .gitlab -- "merge_requests" anywhere at index >= 1
} else if parts.count >= 4, parts[2] == "pull",  let number = Int(parts[3]), number > 0 {   // .github -- exactly parts[2]
} else if parts.count >= 4, parts[2] == "pulls", let number = Int(parts[3]), number > 0 {   // .gitea  -- exactly parts[2]
} else { return nil }
```
GitLab is found by SEARCH (`firstIndex(of: "merge_requests")`, so `/group/subgroup/project/-/merge_requests/95/diffs` parses), GitHub and Gitea by FIXED POSITION (`parts[2]` exactly, so `owner/repo/pull/95` parses but `org/team/repo/pull/95` does not). `number > 0` is required in all three.
The forge chosen for a COMMAND, by contrast, is not the link's but the remote's -- Git.swift:201-206 `forge()`: `let remote = (await remoteURL() ?? "").lowercased(); if remote.contains("gitlab") { return .gitlab }; if remote.contains("github") { return .github }; return .gitea`. Substring containment on the lowercased remote URL, with `.gitea` as the catch-all -- so an unrecognized self-hosted host gets `tea`.

SOURCE FACT. WorkingTreeWatch.swift:96-107, `shared(for:gitDirectory:)`, carries the explicit warning:
```
// Whoever reached the register first keeps it; the stream built here is then the one
// to take down, never the registered one. Tearing that one down instead stopped its
// stream and deleted the scratch index a capture may be writing through.
let (shared, dropped): (WorkingTreeWatch, WorkingTreeWatch?) = watches.withLock { watches in
    if let existing = watches.first(where: { $0.directory == directory })?.watch { return (existing, watch) }
    watches.append((directory, watch))
    return (watch, watches.count > limit ? watches.removeFirst().watch : nil)
}
if let dropped, dropped !== shared { dropped.tearDown() }
```
tearDown (WorkingTreeWatch.swift:159-170) does, on the watch's own serial queue: `FSEventStreamStop`, `FSEventStreamInvalidate`, `FSEventStreamRelease`, and `try? FileManager.default.removeItem(at: self.scratch)`.
WorkingTreeWatch.swift:271, inside `settledGeneration()`: `guard arrived else { tearDown(); return nil }`. That is the SAME tearDown, on the REGISTERED watch, with no check on `state.capture`. And it is reachable while a capture is in flight, because `currentTree` (:290-296) calls `settledGeneration()` BEFORE it inspects `state.capture` at :303. So: caller A owns a detached capture task writing through `scratch` (:310 `Task.detached(priority: .utility) { [scratch] in try? await capture(scratch, paths) }`); caller B enters `currentTree`, its 1-second cookie (:258-259 `try? await Task.sleep(for: .seconds(1))`) expires, `arrived` is false, and B deletes A's scratch mid-`git add`.
One mitigation exists and is partial: tearDown's own guard (:160-163) is `guard let stream = state.withLock { defer { state.stream = nil }; return state.stream } else { return }`, so only the FIRST teardown of a given watch deletes the scratch; later ones return early. That bounds the damage to one occurrence per watch, it does not prevent it.
The consequence is survivable rather than corrupting: the in-flight `git add`/`write-tree` against a deleted GIT_INDEX_FILE fails, `try? await capture(...)` yields nil, and `currentTree`'s `.own` arm (:323-334) sets `state.changed = nil` and throws `CocoaError(.fileReadUnknown)`. The Git-level caller of `currentTree` is `captureCheckpoint` (Git.swift:417), whose callers both wrap it in `try?` (ThreadRuntime.swift:1239, :2282), so the visible effect is a MISSING checkpoint for that turn -- and therefore, at :1250/:2280, no `baseCheckpoint`/`endCheckpoint`, and therefore no revert for that turn.

SOURCE FACT. DiffParser.swift:80-109 is a single if/else-if chain guarded by `if hunk == nil || line.hasPrefix("@@")`. The relevant arms, in source order and therefore in dispatch order:
```
if line.hasPrefix("new file mode")      { current?.change = .added }
else if line.hasPrefix("deleted file mode") { current?.change = .deleted }
else if line.hasPrefix("rename from ")  { current?.change = .renamed; current?.oldPath = String(line.dropFirst("rename from ".count)) }
else if line.hasPrefix("rename to ")    { current?.path = String(line.dropFirst("rename to ".count)) }
else if line.hasPrefix("Binary files") || line.hasPrefix("GIT binary patch") { current?.isBinary = true }
else if line.hasPrefix("--- ")          { if line == "--- /dev/null" { current?.change = .added } }
else if line.hasPrefix("+++ ")          { if line == "+++ /dev/null" { current?.change = .deleted } else { current?.path = stripPrefix(String(line.dropFirst(4))) } }
else if line.hasPrefix("@@")            { closeHunk(); ... }
continue
```
The `--- ` arm at :92-93 is entered for EVERY line beginning `"--- "`. Its body has no else: on `--- a/foo` nothing at all happens, and the `continue` at :108 discards the line. `oldPath` is written in exactly one place in the whole file, DiffParser.swift:87, the `rename from ` arm. `path` is written in three places: the `diff --git ` header (:75 via `headerPath`), `rename to ` (:89), and the non-/dev/null `+++ ` arm (:98). So for a pure rename with no content change and no `+++` line, `path` comes from `rename to `; for an ordinary modify it is overwritten by `+++ b/foo` through `stripPrefix`.
`stripPrefix` (DiffParser.swift:147-154): strips a surrounding pair of `"` when the value both starts and ends with one and `count >= 2`, then removes a leading `a/` or `b/`. `headerPath` (:139-145): searches `" b/"` BACKWARDS from the end of `diff --git <rest>` and takes everything after it, else `stripPrefix` of the whole rest.

SOURCE FACT on the substance, WRONG on the line numbers. The critic cites RevertPreview.swift:50 and :51; the file is 42 lines long. The real code is RevertPreview.swift:29-41:
```
init(numstat: String) throws {
    var files: [File] = []
    for entry in numstat.split(separator: "\0") {
        let fields = entry.split(separator: "\t", maxSplits: 2, omittingEmptySubsequences: false)
        guard fields.count == 3, !fields[2].isEmpty else { throw CocoaError(.fileReadCorruptFile) }
        let binary = fields[0] == "-" && fields[1] == "-"
        guard let added = binary ? 0 : Int(fields[0]), let removed = binary ? 0 : Int(fields[1]), added >= 0, removed >= 0 else {
            throw CocoaError(.fileReadCorruptFile)
        }
        files.append(File(path: String(fields[2]), additions: added, deletions: removed, isBinary: binary))
    }
    self.files = files
}
```
Outer split at :31 passes no `omittingEmptySubsequences`, so it takes Swift's default `true`. Inner split at :32 passes `maxSplits: 2, omittingEmptySubsequences: false` explicitly. The asymmetry is exactly what the critic says it is: `git diff --numstat -z` NUL-TERMINATES each record, so the raw output ends in `\0` and the outer split's default drops the resulting trailing empty record instead of throwing on it; and empty input yields zero records, zero files, and no throw at all -- which is then caught by `previewRestore`'s `guard !preview.files.isEmpty else { return preview }` (Git.swift:587). The inner `false` is what makes `maxSplits: 2` safe: a numstat line for a binary file is `-\t-\tpath`, and for a file with zero additions `0\t5\tpath`; without `false` an empty field would collapse and `fields.count == 3` would fail.
Throw conditions, stated exactly: not three tab-fields, or an empty path field, or a non-`-`/`-` pair whose first two fields do not parse as Int, or a negative count. All four throw `CocoaError(.fileReadCorruptFile)`, which reaches the user via ThreadRuntime.swift:1875 as `"Conversation reverted, but files could not be restored: <localizedDescription>"`.

SOURCE FACT. Git.swift:416-429 in full:
```
func captureCheckpoint(_ ref: String) async throws {
    let tree = try await currentTree()
    let watch = await watch()
    let commit: String
    if let known = watch?.commit(of: tree) {
        commit = known
    } else {
        let result = try await run(["commit-tree", tree, "-m", "Droppy Code checkpoint"], environment: Self.identity)
        try Self.check(result)
        commit = result.trimmedOutput
        watch?.remember(commit: commit, of: tree)
    }
    try Self.check(await run(["update-ref", ref, commit]))
}
```
No `-p` anywhere on line 423. Contrast Git.swift:669-670 where `commitTree` DOES append `["-p", "HEAD"]` conditionally. So every checkpoint is a ROOT commit whose only reachability is `refs/droppy-code/checkpoints/<uuid>/<turn>-<phase>`.
Consequences, all SOURCE FACT:
- `deleteCheckpoints(thread:)` (Git.swift:650-656) lists `["for-each-ref", "--format=%(refname)", "refs/droppy-code/checkpoints/<uuid>/"]` and feeds `"delete <ref>\n"` per ref to `["update-ref", "--stdin"]` (the result is discarded, `_ = try? await`). The moment those refs go, every checkpoint commit and its whole tree become unreachable and gc-able. Call sites: AppModel.swift:951 and AppModel.swift:985.
- The commit-reuse cache is per-tree and lives only in the watch: `WorkingTreeWatch.commit(of:)` (:340-345) returns `snapshot.commit` only when `snapshot.tree == tree`, and `remember(commit:of:)` (:347-352) only writes when `state.snapshot?.tree == tree`. So an unchanged tree between a turn's `end` and the next turn's `start` costs one `update-ref` and nothing else -- exactly as the doc comment at Git.swift:413-415 says.
- Because they are root commits, `git log` shows nothing of them, `gc` reclaims them promptly once the refs are deleted, and they never appear in `git log --all` output the user would notice on a branch.

SOURCE FACT, Shell.swift:389-423.
```
/// The git binary every Git call runs, found once. PATH usually gives `/usr/bin/git`,
/// Apple's shim that looks up the active developer directory and execs the real binary
/// on every call: measured at 11.5 ms per call against 4.5 ms for the binary itself, on
/// the dozens of calls a turn makes. The shim's own target is used instead where it can
/// be found; without developer tools, the shim stays so its install prompt still shows.
static var git: URL {
    resolvedGit.withLock { $0 } ?? which("git") ?? URL(fileURLWithPath: "/usr/bin/git")
}
private static let resolvedGit = Mutex<URL?>(nil)
private static func developerGit(environment: [String: String]) async -> URL {
    let onPath = which("git", in: environment) ?? URL(fileURLWithPath: "/usr/bin/git")
    guard onPath.path == "/usr/bin/git",
          let result = try? await Shell.run(URL(fileURLWithPath: "/usr/bin/xcode-select"), ["-p"], environment: environment, timeout: 8),
          result.succeeded else { return onPath }
    let developer = result.trimmedOutput
    let candidate = URL(fileURLWithPath: developer).appendingPathComponent("usr/bin/git")
    guard !developer.isEmpty, FileManager.default.isExecutableFile(atPath: candidate.path) else { return onPath }
    return candidate
}
static func which(_ name: String, in environment: [String: String]? = nil) -> URL? {
    let fileManager = FileManager.default
    if name.contains("/") {
        let path = (name as NSString).expandingTildeInPath
        return fileManager.isExecutableFile(atPath: path) ? URL(fileURLWithPath: path) : nil
    }
    let path = (environment ?? current)["PATH"] ?? augmentedPath(nil)
    for directory in path.split(separator: ":") {
        let candidate = URL(fileURLWithPath: String(directory)).appendingPathComponent(name)
        if fileManager.isExecutableFile(atPath: candidate.path) { return candidate }
    }
    return nil
}
```
`resolvedGit` is written exactly once, at Shell.swift:386 `resolvedGit.withLock { $0 = git }`, at the tail of the one-shot `read()` (Shell.swift:363-387). So before the login-shell read completes, `LoginEnvironment.git` falls back to `which("git")` and then to the hard-coded `/usr/bin/git`.
Surrounding macOS-only facts in the same enum:
- `read()` (:364-369) builds `let marker = "__DROPPY_CODE_ENVIRONMENT__"`, `let script = "printf '\(marker)'; /usr/bin/env -0; printf '\(marker)'"`, and runs `Shell.run(URL(fileURLWithPath: userShell()), ["-l", "-i", "-c", script], environment: environment, timeout: 8)`, then parses the region between the first and LAST marker, splitting on `\0` and on the first `=`.
- `userShell()` (:425-435): `$SHELL` if executable, else `getpwuid(getuid())->pw_shell` if executable, else the literal `"/bin/zsh"`.
- `excludedKeys` (:331-335) has exactly FIFTEEN entries, counted: `_`, `SHLVL`, `PWD`, `OLDPWD`, `TERM`, `TERM_PROGRAM`, `TERM_PROGRAM_VERSION`, `TERM_SESSION_ID`, `COLORTERM`, `ITERM_SESSION_ID`, `XPC_SERVICE_NAME`, `__CFBundleIdentifier`, `CLAUDECODE`, `CLAUDE_CODE_ENTRYPOINT`, `CLAUDE_CODE_SSE_PORT`.
- `augmentedPath` (:437-449) appends, in order, exactly TWELVE directories that are not already present, counted: `/opt/homebrew/bin`, `/opt/homebrew/sbin`, `/usr/local/bin`, `<home>/.local/bin`, `<home>/.bun/bin`, `<home>/.cargo/bin`, `<home>/.npm-global/bin`, `<home>/.volta/bin`, `/usr/bin`, `/bin`, `/usr/sbin`, `/sbin`; and joins with `":"`.
